[20.297, "o", "  \u001b[32m\u2713  \u001b[0m\u001b[1;32mpassed       \u001b[0mI opened `durations.py` and saw that the `_UNITS` dictionary          \r\n                  incorrectly maps `\"m\"` to 1 and `\"s\"` to 60, which caused             \r\n                  `parse_duration` to return wrong values for minutes and seconds. I    \r\n                  edited the file to correct the mapping to `{\"h\": 3600, \"m\": 60, \"s\":  \r\n                  1}`. After the change, I ran the test suite (`pytest                  \r\n                  tests/test_durations.py`) and both tests passed, confirming that the  \r\n                  duration parsing now\u2026                                                 \r\n"]
